{{History of Balisca}}

The '''2021 Baliscan civil unrest''', popularly known as '''Baliscazo''' (from "Balisca" and the [[w:Spanish nouns#Other suffixes|''-azo'' suffix]] of violent augmentation) or the '''Gen Z Revolution''' (''Revolución generación Z'') refers to a series of protests, clashes, and riots between several unorganized groups throughout the [[Baliscan Confederation]]. Violence sporadically occurred predominantly between anti-statist protesters, including anarchists, [[W:Social ecology (Bookchin)|social ecologists]] and [[Accelerationism|accelerationists]]; and pro-reformist, pro-statist, and a small number of right-wing activists. In [[Castejón]], protests progressively became more violent and multifaceted, ultimately leading the [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón|metropolitan council]] to impose a [[W:state of emergency|state of emergency]] in an attempt to combat the unrest. The unrest is considered to be the largest in Baliscan history, involving some 10 million people.

After an online petition posted in June had attracted nearly 4 million signatures, mass demonstrations began on 11 July. The movement was initially motivated by the [[Baliscan duo|death of Silvestre Santmartí]], increasing cost of living, and economic inequality; it asserted that the Baliscan government was no longer working for the well being of the public. The protesters successfully pushed for {{W|Economic liberalization|the rolling back of the market liberalization reforms}}, deeper confederal and [[W:Direct democracy|direct democratic]] reform, {{W|climate justice}} and [[Immigration to Balisca|immigration reform]], among other things. On 13 July, a list of 50 demands was made public and went viral on social media, becoming de facto a structuring basis for the movement, covering a wide range of eclectic topics, mostly related to democracy, and social and fiscal justice. After Secretary General [[Andrea Palacios]] made a televised address on 27 July announcing her resignation from political office, the movement evolved, with a transition towards political {{W|decentralization}} away from Castejón, and {{W|participatory democracy|participatory}} and {{W|grassroots democracy}} became its unique revendication. On the same day, anarchists utilizing fireworks and [[W:Improvised weapon|improvised weapons]] in Castejón's [[Capitoline Hill]] forced the local [[Mozos de Escuadra]] to retreat, and declared the first autonomous commune.

Deputy Secretary General [[Zavier Bilal]] assumed the duties as SecGen immediately after [[Andrea Palacios]]' resignation. Sizeable portions of the Baliscan public, especially from the right wing, and some members of the international community alike called for the next confederal elections to be moved ahead; which ultimately [[2021 Baliscan confederal election|occurred in October]]. As a result of the elections, the {{W|neoliberal}} [[Reformist Bloc (Balisca)|Reformist Bloc]] lost all of it's seats, while the number {{W|moderate}} and {{W|conservative}} delegates elected was significantly reduced, winning elections in only 23 [[Commune district|commune districts]] (out of 736), and the electorate rallying around {{W|anti-capitalist}}, {{W|anti-statism|anti-statist}} and [[Baliscan Accelerationism|accelerationist]] candidates. It was under Bilal's government that the referendum to approve the new Constitution was held on 23 July 2022, which pass with the support of 66% of the electorate.

